window.docContents[252].resolve({"/Functors___-Monads-and--do--Notation/Syntax/#do-control-structures":{"contents":"There are do elements that correspond to most of Lean's term-level control structures.\nWhen they occur as a step in a do block, they are interpreted as do elements rather than terms.\nEach branch of the control structures is a sequence of do elements, rather than a term, and some of them are more syntactically flexible than their corresponding terms.\n\nConditionalsIn a do block, if statements may omit their else branch.\nOmitting an else branch is equivalent to using pure () as the contents of the branch.\n\nSyntactically, the then branch cannot be omitted.\nFor these cases, unless only executes its body when the condition is false.\nThe do in unless is part of its syntax and does not induce a nested do block.\n\nReverse Conditionals\n\nWhen match is used in a do block, each branch is considered to be part of the same block.\nOtherwise, it is equivalent to the match term.\n\nPattern Matching\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Functors, Monads and  do -Notation\u0009Syntax\u0009do -Notation","header":"19.3.2.4. Control Structures","id":"/Functors___-Monads-and--do--Notation/Syntax/#do-control-structures"},"/IO/Async/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--IO--Asynchronous-Programming--Cancellation--Cancellation-Contexts":{"contents":"ContextAsync is a reader on top of Async that provides access to a cancellation context.\nThis context contains an ID and a cancellation token along with a mutex-guarded mutable state that encodes a tree of IDs, each with a cancellation token, and a source of unique ID values.\nWhen child tasks are created, they are assigned new IDs and associated with the current task.\nWhen tasks are canceled, the tree in the state is used to cancel their children.\n\nA cancellation context that allows multiple consumers to wait until cancellation is requested. Forms\na tree structure where cancelling a parent cancels all children.\n\nMap of token IDs to optional tokens and their children.Next available ID\n\nCreates a new root cancellation context.\n\nForks a child context from a parent. If the parent is already cancelled, returns the parent context.\nOtherwise, creates a new child that will be cancelled when the parent is cancelled.\n\nCancels this context and all child contexts with the given reason.\n\nChecks if the context is cancelled.\n\nReturns the cancellation reason if the context is cancelled.\n\nWaits for cancellation. Returns a task that completes when the context is cancelled.\n\nCreates a selector that waits for cancellation.\n\nReasons for cancellation.Cancelled due to a deadline or timeoutCancelled due to shutdownExplicitly cancelledCustom cancellation reason\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009IO\u0009Asynchronous Programming\u0009Cancellation","header":"22.12.8.2. Cancellation Contexts","id":"/IO/Async/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--IO--Asynchronous-Programming--Cancellation--Cancellation-Contexts"},"/IO/Processes/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--IO--Processes--Running-Processes":{"contents":"There are three primary ways to run other programs from Lean:\n\n1. IO.Process.run synchronously executes another program, returning its standard output as a string. It throws an error if the process exits with an error code other than 0.2. IO.Process.output synchronously executes another program with an empty standard input, capturing its standard output, standard error, and exit code. No error is thrown if the process terminates unsuccessfully.3. IO.Process.spawn starts another program asynchronously and returns a data structure that can be used to access the process's standard input, output, and error streams.\n\nRuns a process to completion, blocking until it terminates.\nThe child process is run with a null standard input or the specified input if provided,\nIf the child process terminates successfully with exit code 0, its standard output is returned.\nAn exception is thrown if it terminates with any other exit code.The specifications of standard input, output, and error handles in args are ignored.\n\nRunning a ProgramWhen run, this program concatenates its own source code with itself twice using the Unix tool cat.-- Main.lean begins here\ndef main : IO Unit := do\n  let src2 ← IO.Process.run {cmd := \"cat\", args := #[\"Main.lean\", \"Main.lean\"]}\n  IO.println src2\n-- Main.lean ends here\nIts output is:-- Main.lean begins here\ndef main : IO Unit := do\n  let src2 ← IO.Process.run {cmd := \"cat\", args := #[\"Main.lean\", \"Main.lean\"]}\n  IO.println src2\n-- Main.lean ends here\n-- Main.lean begins here\ndef main : IO Unit := do\n  let src2 ← IO.Process.run {cmd := \"cat\", args := #[\"Main.lean\", \"Main.lean\"]}\n  IO.println src2\n-- Main.lean ends here\n\n\nRunning a Program on a FileThis program uses the Unix utility grep as a filter to find four-digit palindromes.\nIt creates a file that contains all numbers from 0 through 9999, and then invokes grep on it, reading the result from its standard output.def main : IO Unit := do\n  -- Feed the input to the subprocess\n  IO.FS.withFile \"numbers.txt\" .write fun h =>\n    for i in [0:10000] do\n      h.putStrLn (toString i)\n\n  let palindromes ← IO.Process.run {\n    cmd := \"grep\",\n    args := #[r#\"^\\([0-9]\\)\\([0-9]\\)\\2\\1$\"#, \"numbers.txt\"]\n  }\n\n  let count := palindromes.trimAscii.split \"\\n\" |>.length\n\n  IO.println s!\"There are {count} four-digit palindromes.\"\nIts output is:There are 90 four-digit palindromes.\n\n\nRuns a process to completion and captures its output and exit code.\nThe child process is run with a null standard input or the specified input if provided,\nand the current process blocks until it has run to completion.The specifications of standard input, output, and error handles in args are ignored.\n\nChecking Exit CodesWhen run, this program first invokes cat on a nonexistent file and displays the resulting error code.\nIt then concatenates its own source code with itself twice using the Unix tool cat.-- Main.lean begins here\ndef main : IO UInt32 := do\n  let src1 ← IO.Process.output {cmd := \"cat\", args := #[\"Nonexistent.lean\"]}\n  IO.println s!\"Exit code from failed process: {src1.exitCode}\"\n\n  let src2 ← IO.Process.output {cmd := \"cat\", args := #[\"Main.lean\", \"Main.lean\"]}\n  if src2.exitCode == 0 then\n    IO.println src2.stdout\n  else\n    IO.eprintln \"Concatenation failed\"\n    return 1\n\n  return 0\n-- Main.lean ends here\nIts output is:Exit code from failed process: 1\n-- Main.lean begins here\ndef main : IO UInt32 := do\n  let src1 ← IO.Process.output {cmd := \"cat\", args := #[\"Nonexistent.lean\"]}\n  IO.println s!\"Exit code from failed process: {src1.exitCode}\"\n\n  let src2 ← IO.Process.output {cmd := \"cat\", args := #[\"Main.lean\", \"Main.lean\"]}\n  if src2.exitCode == 0 then\n    IO.println src2.stdout\n  else\n    IO.eprintln \"Concatenation failed\"\n    return 1\n\n  return 0\n-- Main.lean ends here\n-- Main.lean begins here\ndef main : IO UInt32 := do\n  let src1 ← IO.Process.output {cmd := \"cat\", args := #[\"Nonexistent.lean\"]}\n  IO.println s!\"Exit code from failed process: {src1.exitCode}\"\n\n  let src2 ← IO.Process.output {cmd := \"cat\", args := #[\"Main.lean\", \"Main.lean\"]}\n  if src2.exitCode == 0 then\n    IO.println src2.stdout\n  else\n    IO.eprintln \"Concatenation failed\"\n    return 1\n\n  return 0\n-- Main.lean ends here\n\n\n\nStarts a child process with the provided configuration. The child process is spawned using operating\nsystem primitives, and it can be written in any language.The child process runs in parallel with the parent.If the child process's standard input is a pipe, use IO.Process.Child.takeStdin to make it\npossible to close the child's standard input before the process terminates, which provides the child with an end-of-file marker.\n\nAsynchronous SubprocessesThis program uses the Unix utility grep as a filter to find four-digit palindromes.\nIt feeds all numbers from 0 through 9999 to the grep process and then reads its result.\nThis code is only correct when grep is sufficiently fast and when the output pipe is large enough to contain all 90 four-digit palindromes.def main : IO Unit := do\n  let grep ← IO.Process.spawn {\n    cmd := \"grep\",\n    args := #[r#\"^\\([0-9]\\)\\([0-9]\\)\\2\\1$\"#],\n    stdin := .piped,\n    stdout := .piped,\n    stderr := .null\n  }\n\n  -- Feed the input to the subprocess\n  for i in [0:10000] do\n    grep.stdin.putStrLn (toString i)\n\n  -- Consume its output, after waiting 100ms for grep to process the data.\n  IO.sleep 100\n  let count := (← grep.stdout.readToEnd).trimAscii.split \"\\n\" |>.length\n\n  IO.println s!\"There are {count} four-digit palindromes.\"\nIts output is:There are 90 four-digit palindromes.\n\n\nConfiguration for a child process to be spawned.Use IO.Process.spawn to start the child process. IO.Process.output and IO.Process.run can be\nused when the child process should be run to completion, with its output and/or error code captured.Command name.Arguments for the command.The child process's working directory. Inherited from the parent current process if none.Add or remove environment variables for the child process.The child process inherits the parent's environment, as modified by env. Keys in the array are\nthe names of environment variables. A none, causes the entry to be removed from the environment,\nand some sets the variable to the new value, adding it if necessary. Variables are processed from left to right.Inherit environment variables from the spawning process.Starts the child process in a new session and process group using setsid. Currently a no-op on\nnon-POSIX platforms.\n\nConfiguration for the standard input, output, and error handles of a child process.Configuration for the process' stdin handle.Configuration for the process' stdout handle.Configuration for the process' stderr handle.\n\nWhether the standard input, output, and error handles of a child process should be attached to\npipes, inherited from the parent, or null.If the stream is a pipe, then the parent process can use it to communicate with the child.The stream should be attached to a pipe.The stream should be inherited from the parent process.The stream should be empty.\n\nThe type of handles that can be used to communicate with a child process on its standard input,\noutput, or error streams.For IO.Process.Stdio.piped, this type is IO.FS.Handle. Otherwise, it is Unit, because no\ncommunication is possible.\n\nA child process that was spawned with configuration cfg.The configuration determines whether the child process's standard input, standard output, and\nstandard error are IO.FS.Handles or Unit.The child process's standard input handle, if it was configured as IO.Process.Stdio.piped, or\n() otherwise.The child process's standard output handle, if it was configured as IO.Process.Stdio.piped, or\n() otherwise.The child process's standard error handle, if it was configured as IO.Process.Stdio.piped, or\n() otherwise.\n\nBlocks until the child process has exited and returns its exit code.\n\nChecks whether the child has exited. Returns none if the process has not exited, or its exit code\nif it has.\n\nTerminates the child process using the SIGTERM signal or a platform analogue.If the process was started using SpawnArgs.setsid, terminates the entire process group instead.\n\nExtracts the stdin field from a Child object, allowing the handle to be closed while maintaining\na reference to the child process.File handles are closed when the last reference to them is dropped. Closing the child's standard\ninput causes an end-of-file marker. Because the Child object has a reference to the standard\ninput, this operation is necessary in order to close the stream while the process is running (e.g.\nto extract its exit code after calling Child.wait). Many processes do not terminate until their\nstandard input is exhausted.\n\nClosing a Subprocess's Standard InputThis program uses the Unix utility grep as a filter to find four-digit palindromes, ensuring that the subprocess terminates successfully.\nIt feeds all numbers from 0 through 9999 to the grep process, then closes the process's standard input, which causes it to terminate.\nAfter checking grep's exit code, the program extracts its result.def main : IO UInt32 := do\n  let grep ← do\n    let (stdin, child) ← (← IO.Process.spawn {\n      cmd := \"grep\",\n      args := #[r#\"^\\([0-9]\\)\\([0-9]\\)\\2\\1$\"#],\n      stdin := .piped,\n      stdout := .piped,\n      stderr := .null\n    }).takeStdin\n\n    -- Feed the input to the subprocess\n    for i in [0:10000] do\n      stdin.putStrLn (toString i)\n\n    -- Return the child without its stdin handle.\n    -- This closes the handle, because there are\n    -- no more references to it.\n    pure child\n\n  -- Wait for grep to terminate\n  if (← grep.wait) != 0 then\n    IO.eprintln s!\"grep terminated unsuccessfully\"\n    return 1\n\n  -- Consume its output\n  let count := (← grep.stdout.readToEnd).trimAscii.split \"\\n\" |>.length\n\n  IO.println s!\"There are {count} four-digit palindromes.\"\n  return 0\nIts output is:There are 90 four-digit palindromes.\n\n\nThe result of running a process to completion.The process's exit code.Everything that was written to the process's standard output.Everything that was written to the process's standard error.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009IO\u0009Processes","header":"22.9.2. Running Processes","id":"/IO/Processes/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--IO--Processes--Running-Processes"},"/releases/v4.24.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___24___0-_LPAR_2025-10-14_RPAR_--Library":{"contents":"* #7858 implements the fast circuit for overflow detection in unsigned\nmultiplication used by Bitwuzla and proposed in:\nhttps://ieeexplore.ieee.org/stamp/stamp.jsp?tp=&arnumber=987767* #9127 makes saveModuleData throw an IO.Error instead of panicking,\nif given something that cannot be serialized. This doesn't really matter\nfor saving modules, but is handy when writing tools to save auxiliary\ndata in olean files via Batteries' pickle.* #9560 fixes the forIn function, that previously caused the resulting\nPromise to be dropped without a value when an exception was thrown\ninside of it. It also corrects the parameter order of the background\nfunction.* #9599 adds the type Std.Internal.Parsec.Error, which contains the\nconstructors .eof (useful for checking if parsing failed due to not\nhaving enough input and then retrying when more input arrives that is\nuseful in the HTTP server) and .other, which describes other errors.\nIt also adds documentation to many functions, along with some new\nfunctions to the ByteArray Parsec, such as peekWhen?, octDigit,\ntakeWhile, takeUntil, skipWhile, and skipUntil.* #9632 adds lemmas for the TreeMap operations filter, map and\nfilterMap. These lemmas existed already for hash maps and are simply\nported over from there.* #9685 verifies toArray and related functions for hashmaps.* #9797 provides the means to quickly provide all the order instances\nassociated with some high-level order structure (preorder, partial\norder, linear preorder, linear order). This can be done via the factory\nfunctions PreorderPackage.ofLE, PartialOrderPackage.ofLE,\nLinearPreorderPackage.ofLE and LinearOrderPackage.ofLE.* #9908 makes IsPreorder, IsPartialOrder, IsLinearPreorder and\nIsLinearOrder extend BEq and Ord as appropriate, adds the\nLawfulOrderBEq and LawfulOrderOrd type classes relating BEq and\nOrd to LE, and adds many lemmas and instances.* #9916 provides factories that derive order type classes in bulk, given\nan Ord instance. If present, existing instances are preferred over\nthose derived from Ord. It is possible to specify any instance\nmanually if desired.* #9924 fixes examples in the documentation for PostCond.* #9931 implements Std.Do.Triple.mp, enabling users to compose two\nspecifications for the same program.* #9949 allows most of the List.lookup lemmas to be used when\nLawfulBEq α is not available.* #9957 upstreams the definition of Rat from Batteries, for use in our\nplanned interval arithmetic tactic.* #9967 removes local Triple notation from SpecLemmas.lean to work\naround a bug that breaks the stage2 build.* #9979 replaces Std.Internal.Rat with the new public Rat upstreamed\nfrom Batteries.* #9987 improves the tactic for proving that elements of a Nat-based\nPRange are in-bounds by relying on the omega tactic.* #9993 defines the dyadic rationals, showing they are an ordered ring\nembedding into the rationals. We will use this for future interval\narithmetic tactics.* #9999 reduces the number of Nat.Bitwise grind annotations we have\nthe deal with distributivity. The new smaller set encourages grind to\nrewrite into DNF. The old behaviour just resulted in saturating up to\nthe instantiation limits.* #10000 removes a grind annotation that fired on all Option.maps,\ncausing an avalanche of instantiations.* #10005 shortens the work necessary to make a type compatible with the\npolymorphic range notation. In the concrete case of Nat, it reduces\nthe required lines of code from 150 to 70.* #10015 exposes the bodies of Name.append, Name.appendCore, and\nName.hasMacroScopes. This enables proof by reflection of the\nconcatenation of name literals when using the module system.* #10018 derives BEq and Hashable for Lean.Import. Lake already did\nthis later, but it now done when defining Import.* #10019 adds @[expose] to Lean.ParserState.setPos. This makes it\npossible to prove in-boundedness for a state produced by setPos for\nfunctions like next' and get' without needing to import all.* #10024 adds useful declarations to the LawfulOrderMin/Max and\nLawfulOrderLeftLeaningMin/Max API. In particular, it introduces\n.leftLeaningOfLE factories for Min and Max. It also renames\nLawfulOrderMin/Max.of_le to .of_le_min_iffand.of_max_le_iff` and\nintroduces a second variant with different arguments.* #10045 implements the necessary type classes so that range notation\nworks for integers. For example, ((-2)...3).toList = [-2, -1, 0, 1, 2] : List Int.* #10049 adds some background material needed for introducing the dyadic\nrationals in #9993.* #10050 fixes some naming issues in Data/Rat/Lemmas, and upstreams the\neliminator numDenCasesOn and its relatives.* #10059 improves the names of definitions and lemmas in the polymorphic\nrange API. It also introduces a recommended spelling. For example, a\nleft-closed, right-open range is spelled Rco in analogy with Mathlib's\nIco intervals.* #10075 contains lemmas about Int (minor amendments for BitVec and\nNat) that are being used in preparing the dyadics. This is all work of\n@Rob23oba, which I'm pulling out of #9993 early to keep that one\nmanageable.* #10077 upstreams lemmas about Rat from Mathlib.Data.Rat.Defs and\nMathlib.Algebra.Order.Ring.Unbundled.Rat, specifically enough to get\nLean.Grind.Field Rat and Lean.Grind.OrderedRing Rat. In addition to\nthe lemmas, instances for Inv Rat, Pow Rat Nat and Pow Rat Int\nhave been upstreamed.* #10107 adds the Lean.Grind.AddCommGroup instance for Rat.* #10138 adds lemmas about the Dyadic.roundUp and Dyadic.roundDown\noperations.* #10159 adds nodup_keys lemmas as corollaries of existing\ndistinct_keys to all Map variants.* #10162 removes grind → annotations that fire too often, unhelpfully.\nIt would be nice for grind to instantiate these lemmas, but only if\nthey already see xs ++ ys and #[] in the same equivalence class, not\njust as soon as it sees xs ++ ys.* #10163 removes some (hopefully) unnecessary grind annotations that\ncause instantiation explosions.* #10173 removes the extends Monad from MonadAwait and MonadAsync\nto avoid underdetermined instances.* #10182 adds lemmas about Nat.fold and Nat.foldRev on sums, to match\nthe existing theorems about dfold and dfoldRev.* #10194 adds the inverse of a dyadic rational, at a given precision, and\ncharacterising lemmas. Also cleans up various parts of the Int.DivMod\nand Rat APIs, and proves some characterising lemmas about\nRat.toDyadic.* #10216 fixes #10193.* #10224 generalizes the monadic operations for HashMap, TreeMap, and\nHashSet to work for m : Type u → Type v.* #10227 adds @[grind] annotations (nearly all @[grind =] annotations\nparallel to existing @[simp]s) for ReaderT, StateT, ExceptT.* #10244 adds more lemmas about the toList and toArray functions on\nranges and iterators. It also renames Array.mem_toArray into\nList.mem_toArray.* #10247 adds missing the lemmas ofList_eq_insertMany_empty,\nget?_eq_some_iff, getElem?_eq_some_iff and getKey?_eq_some_iff to\nall container types.* #10250 fixes a bug in the LinearOrderPackage.ofOrd factory. If there\nis a LawfulEqOrd instance available, it should automatically use it\ninstead of requiring the user to provide the eq_of_compare argument to\nthe factory. The PR also solves a hygiene-related problem making the\nfactories fail when Std is not open.* #10303 adds range support toBitVec and the UInt* types. This means\nthat it is now possible to write, for example, for i in (1 : UInt8)...5 do, in order to loop over the values 1, 2, 3 and 4 of type UInt8.* #10341 moves the definitions and basic facts about Function.Injective\nand Function.Surjective up from Mathlib. We can do a better job of\narguing via injectivity in grind if these are available.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Release Notes\u0009Lean 4.24.0 (2025-10-14)","header":"Library","id":"/releases/v4.24.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___24___0-_LPAR_2025-10-14_RPAR_--Library"},"/releases/v4.36.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___36___0-_LPAR_2026-11-16_RPAR_--Tactics":{"contents":"* #15750\nfixes two grind regressions introduced by #15420 and reported in leanprover/cslib#1141, where proofs about inductive predicates over λ-terms that v4.35.0 proved fail on v4.36.0-rc1. The normalizer now rewrites ∀ _ : α, True to True for every α, not only for propositions, and the eager cases that grind applies to new hypotheses resets the E-matching round counter like any other case split.* #15678\nfixes grind failing to prove goals such as c = b → a ≤ b + c + d → a ≤ b + b + d in an ordered ring when the Sym.simp normalizer is enabled (set_option backward.grind.normalizer false). The normalizer rewrites b + b + d to 2 * b + d, so the two inequalities are no longer congruent after c = b, and no module related the two sums.* #15675\nfixes grind failing with the internal error \"structure does not have a lawful LT instance\" (or \"structure does not support LT\") when linarith has to reason about < on a type without a LawfulOrderLT instance. For example, [IntModule α] [LE α] [LT α] [IsLinearOrder α] [OrderedAdd α] (a b c : α) : a < b → b < c → a < c now fails with a regular grind failed message.* #15672\nfixes grind failing to use k⁻¹ for a numeral k in a field when the Sym.simp normalizer is enabled (set_option backward.grind.normalizer false). For example, [Field R] [IsCharP R 0] (h : f (a * 2⁻¹) = 1) (h2 : a = 2 * b) : f b = 1 was not proved.* #15642\nadds the option Grind.Config.distrib (default false) to the Sym.simp-based grind normalizer. By default, a product of two non-constant polynomials such as x * (y + 1), a power of a sum, and the inverse of a product are kept as atoms whose factors are normalized, as the legacy normalizer does; grind +distrib expands them into polynomial normal form. Linear arithmetic procedures treat nonlinear terms as atoms, so keeping them unexpanded preserves facts such as x * (y + 1) = x * z after y + 1 = z is derived.* #15624\nmakes grind put k#w literals with a symbolic width w in the OfNat.ofNat form it uses for BitVec literals, so with the Sym.simp-based normalizer theorems such as BitVec.sdiv_zero apply to x.sdiv 0 as they do with the legacy normalizer.* #15623\nmakes the Sym.simp-based grind normalizer rewrite dependent propositional binder domains, so ∀ h : i < a.size, q h becomes ∀ h : i + 1 ≤ a.size, ... as with the legacy normalizer.* #15619\nfixes a panic in the Sym.simp-based grind normalizer on match equations whose patterns contain arithmetic, e.g. grind [h.eq_def] for a function matching on Vec α (n + 1 + 1).* #15618\nfixes spurious ring term with unexpected instance issues in grind for casts over abstract rings, e.g. (m + n) • a = m • a + n • a with [CommRing R], and the corresponding failures of the Sym.simp-based normalizer.* #15616\nadds BitVec.zero, BitVec.ofNat _ (Bool.toNat _), and BitVec.carry to the list of supported terms by bv_decide* #15612\nfixes grind losing a hypothesis whose type is marked [grind cases eager] when the hypothesis is introduced by intros or asserted as a parameter fact. Only the constructor fields survived in each branch, so example : P → ¬P → False := by grind and grind [(mkP)] on the goal P failed for a predicate P with field-less constructors.* #15611\nfixes grind? failing with \"proof contains unresolved internal metavariable\" when a parameter fact has a type marked [grind cases eager]. Asserting such a fact splits the goal during initialization, and grind? only produced a script for the first goal. It now handles every goal, and the suggested script carries one · block per goal.* #15609\nmakes grind [mkP] accept a theorem whose statement is not a ∀ and is not indexable, e.g., theorem mkP : P for a nullary inductive predicate P. The parameter is asserted as a fact, so the workaround grind [(mkP)] is no longer needed. grind? and finish? keep such identifiers in their suggestions.* #15606\nfixes a spurious \"generated tactic cannot close the goal\" warning from finish? when its parameters alone close the goal, e.g. grind => finish? [inj f_inj].* #15602\nfixes grind? and finish? dropping parameters such as inj f_inj and funCC q from their suggestions, which then failed when pasted.* #15600\nfixes grind failing on instances wrapped in @[implicit_reducible] definitions.* #15599\nfixes a grind internal error, ring term has not been internalized, triggered when the ring solver learned a disequality such as a ≠ b while a term containing a - b was still being internalized. The Rabinowitsch step then built (a - b) * (a - b)⁻¹ and internalized it before (a - b)⁻¹ had an ENode. Closes #15590.* #15589\nfixes a panic in grind ring when propagating implied equalities in rings without NoNatZeroDivisors, such as BitVec n.* #15588\nfixes a grind internal error (ring term has not been internalized) triggered when a user-defined Pow Int Nat instance with higher priority is in scope. Mathlib's Monoid.npow instance on Int hits this case after #15506.* #15555\nmakes vcgen take the program type and the WP instance from the goal's wp application when it applies a spec. Thus vcgen works on a goal whose program is not of the instance's program type, for example a program e : Exp in a goal wp (Prog := Tagged n Exp) e Q E for def Tagged (n : Nat) (α : Type) := α.* #15582\nmakes cbv evaluate cond (bif c then a else b) lazily, like ite and dite. Before this change, cbv evaluated both branches of cond, even when the condition was the literal true. An expensive or non-terminating branch that the condition does not select cost time or exhausted cbv.maxSteps. The fix gives the cond simproc pattern all four arguments of cond.* #15497\nadds a guard to WP.Frames, so that vcgen can frame a resource across a program whose footprint depends on the state.* #15526\nfixes a kernel error when sym => simp rewrites inside a non-dependent arrow (e.g. x + 0 = y → p) in a file using module.* #15515\nmakes vcgen split a spec precondition fun _ => ∃ h : i < xs.size, Q () xs[i] into the VC i < xs.size and the entailment for Q () xs[i] under a hypothesis h, so that the wp of the program tail in the body is stepped. The precondition fun _ => ⨆ h : i < xs.size, Q () xs[i] splits the same way.* #15524\nfixes a grind internal error (unknown free variable) on hints such as grind [h _ _ pf] whose only hole is a proof. It broke List.pairwise_iff_forall_infix in Mathlib after #15506.* #15506\nfixes a grind internal error (semiring term has not been internalized) triggered when power propagation rebuilds a term whose arguments are still being internalized (#15479).* #15503\nfixes sym with arith and grind with the Sym.simp-based normalizer on goals whose equations are over a type in Sort u, such as {α : Sort u} (a b : α). The ring classifier computed the universe level of the carrier with getDecLevel, which fails with \"invalid universe level, u is not greater than 0\" for such types; it now uses getDecLevel? and reports that the type has no ring structure, as the order classifier already did.* #15491\nmakes vcgen recognize more @[spec] preconditions as conjunctive in the schematic postcondition Q/E. vcgen applies such a spec directly and runs no frame inference for it.* #15487\nmakes vcgen split a pointwise ∧ in a spec's precondition like a lattice meet ⊓.* #15483\nmakes Sym.simp rewrite a raw Nat literal that is not the argument of a numeral into the OfNat.ofNat form, as Meta.simp does. Rewrite rules whose pattern variable binds the raw literal of a numeral, such as the [grind hom] rule (OfNat.ofNat a : Fin n).val = a % n, produced terms like nat_lit 0 % (p + 1) that no later step recognized as a numeral, so grind with the Sym.simp-based normalizer failed on goals over Fin (p + 1) and BitVec n.* #15482\nmakes grind prove goals over UIntN and BitVec that multiply by a numeral close to the modulus, such as -1 * a or 4294967295 * a over UInt32; these previously exhausted the lia step budget. The [grind hom] injection into Nat turned (c * a).toNat into c * a.toNat % 2^w, and cutsat splits on the coefficient c when it has to eliminate a.toNat exactly, while (-a).toNat is (2^w - a.toNat) % 2^w, with coefficient 1.* #15478\nfixes a kernel-rejected proof in grind's ring module. For an inverse a⁻¹ with a numeral a, processInv asserts a * a⁻¹ = 1 or a⁻¹ = 0 with theorems whose numeral is denoteInt k, i.e. an OfNat numeral or its negation, but the recognizer also accepted ↑(0 : Nat), ↑(3 : Int), -0, and - -3, which are only propositionally equal to denoteInt k; Field.inv_zero : 0⁻¹ = 0 was then pushed as a proof of (↑0)⁻¹ = ↑0.* #15476\nmakes the Sym arithmetic normalizer rewrite a cast of a numeral (↑(0 : Nat), ↑(-2 : Int)) to the numeral of the carrier wherever it occurs, as the legacy grind normalizer does. Casts were interpreted only inside +, *, ...; a cast standing alone, as a function argument or under ⁻¹, was kept, so (↑0)⁻¹ reached the ring solver of grind, which applied Field.inv_zero to it and produced a proof rejected by the kernel (grind_9321 with backward.grind.normalizer := false).* #15475\nmakes the Sym.simp-based grind normalizer rewrite every Fin literal to its canonical form. A literal such as @OfNat.ofNat (Fin (1 + 1)) 0 Fin.instOfNat, with a nested numeral as value and a ground type index, was left as is, and grind treated it as a literal distinct from (0 : Fin 2), producing proofs rejected by the kernel (grind_canon_ofnat).* #15473\nadds a Sym.dsimp pass after Sym.simp in the Sym.simp-based grind normalizer, so that beta-redexes and projections of structure instances in the fixed arguments of an application are reduced, as the legacy normalizer does with Simp.Config.dsimp. Sym.simp does not visit these arguments, and a redex left in the implicit objects of a morphism composition made the goals of Functor.flip in grind_cat.lean miss NatTrans.naturality.* #15467\nmakes the projection reduction of Sym (Sym.reduceProjApp?, used by Sym.simp, Sym.dsimp, and the canonicalizer) expose the structure argument at .reducible transparency, as simp does, instead of the ambient one. With the Sym.simp-based grind normalizer, E-matching patterns are normalized at attribute time at .default transparency, so a pattern such as (asIso f).hom unfolded asIso and collapsed to a variable, and the theorem was rejected with invalid pattern.* #15466\nfixes two ways a grind congruence table entry could go stale, which made grind.debug fail nondeterministically on goals with match conditions, and adds a deterministic invariant check for the table.* #15465\nmakes the Sym.simp-based grind normalizer respect the annotations on match equations: Grind.simpMatchDiscrsOnly (only the discriminants of the match are simplified) and Grind.PreMatchCond (the condition is left as is). E-matching instances of match equations, e.g. from f.eq_def, now work with backward.grind.normalizer := false, where they used to fail or produce proofs rejected by the kernel.* #15464\nadds the option backward.grind.normalizer. It defaults to true, which selects the legacy simp-based grind normalizer; with false, grind normalizes hypotheses, the target, E-matching instances, generalized patterns, and E-matching patterns at attribute time with the Sym.simp-based normalizer developed with grind_norm. The default behavior is unchanged.* #15463\nextends Sym.Simp.evalGround to evaluate Int.toNat, Int.natAbs, and toNat on UInt8, UInt16, UInt32, and UInt64 literals, e.g. (-7 : Int).natAbs becomes 7. grind is affected too, since the [grind hom] rewriter uses evalGround.* #15461\nmakes the realized constants required by bv_decide private to avoid name clashes while\nimporting multiple modules that realized them.* #15460\nextends Sym.Simp.evalGround to evaluate String.push and String.singleton on literals, e.g. \"abc\".push 'd' becomes \"abcd\". grind is affected too, since the [grind hom] rewriter uses evalGround.* #15458\nmakes the Sym.simp-based grind normalizer produce bit-vector literals in OfNat.ofNat form, the form grind keeps, instead of BitVec.ofNat. Ground bit-vector terms such as 3#8 + 5#8, 3 &&& 5, (3#8).zeroExtend 16, and 300#8 are now evaluated to the same literals as the legacy normalizer.* #15448\nfixes a kernel error in grind on goals mentioning both Char.ofNat 97 and 'a', and makes grind evaluate Char.toNat, Char.val, and Char.ofNat when the argument is equal to a literal, e.g. y = 'a' → y.toNat = 97 and n = 97 → Char.ofNat n = 'a'.* #15445\nmakes grind prove goals about Fin.log2 and about the scoped Nat and Int casts into Fin n through their val interpretation, such as (x.log2).val = Nat.log2 x.val, ((i : Int) : Fin n).val = (i % n).toNat, and ((k : Nat) : Fin n).val = k % n.* #15434\nmakes the Sym.Arith normalizer solve equations over commutative rings with a nonzero characteristic, such as Fin n, UInt8, and BitVec w, when it runs in the mode used by the grind normalizer. An equation k * m + k' = 0 or k * m₁ = k * m₂ whose coefficient k is invertible modulo the characteristic is solved for the monomial: in Fin 5, 4 * x + 2 = 0 becomes x = 2, as 2 * x = 6 already becomes x = 3 over Int.* #15433\nextends Sym.Simp.evalGround to evaluate the Fin functions on literals: succ, castSucc, rev, last, val, pred, castAdd, addNat, natAdd, castLT, castLE, subNat, mk, and ofNat. It also normalizes Fin literals: (7 : Fin 5) becomes 2, and a type index that is not a numeral, as in (4 : Fin (5 + 1)), is evaluated. Fin arithmetic and comparisons were already supported.* #15398\nintroduces a CEGAR/Lemmas on Demand procedure into bv_decide for supporting uninterpreted functions even without interacting with grind mode. Towards this it refactors the solving pipeline to be fully incremental and makes use of incremental SAT solving. The congruence feature is available behind bv_decide +uf, it is currently limited to:1. Only functions that return Bool/BitVec2. Only performing congruence reasoning on non-dependent Bool/BitVec arguments (see the bv_uf test for examples of what this entails)* #15420\nfixes a grind regression introduced by #15378: a goal such as Imp (∀ i, i < n → ∃ j, j = i + 1 ∧ p j) (p 4), where Imp a b unfolds to a → b through a [grind norm] rule, was no longer proved, while the same goal written with a syntactic → still was. grind now normalizes the goal before negating it, and the normalizer no longer rewrites (∀ x, p x) → q into (∃ x, ¬p x) ∨ q.* #15419\nextends Sym.Simp.evalGround to evaluate Char.val on character literals, e.g. 'a'.val becomes 97. It complements #15411, which added the other Char operations.* #15417\nfixes an Application type mismatch error in grind when the homomorphism rewriter translates an equality whose hypothesis mentions a let-bound literal, e.g. b = m.cpop with let m : BitVec 2 := 1#2. Closes #15388.* #15411\nextends Sym.Simp.evalGround to evaluate Char operations on character literals: toNat, toLower, toUpper, toString, isWhitespace, isUpper, isLower, isAlpha, isDigit, isAlphanum, ==, and !=. It also converts Char.ofNat n into a character literal when n is a numeral, so that a character has a single representation.* #15409\nadds Sym.Simp.reduceControl, a variant of simpControl that does not block the simplifier: it simplifies the condition of ite/dite/cond and the discriminants of match, reduces the term when they are decided, and otherwise lets the simplifier visit the branches. The Sym.simp-based grind normalizer uses it in place of reduceMatcher.* #15405\nfixes a sharing violation in the arith simproc of Sym.simp, e.g., on a + f (b + 0). With sym.debug enabled, it surfaced as a panic in assertShared.* #15322\nmakes Sym.intros introduce a binder whose name starts with __ as an implementation-detail local, as the elaborator does for such binders. vcgen introduces the join point __do_jp of a do block with Sym.intros, so its verification conditions no longer show the join point and its body in the local context.* #15389\nfixes a sharing violation in Sym.dsimp when it descends into a fun, ∀, or let binder whose bound variable occurs in the body or in a later binder type. With sym.debug enabled, the violation surfaced as a panic in assertShared.* #15383\nadds the fallback modifier to the [grind hom] attribute. A fallback rule is applied to a term only when no other homomorphism rule matches it, using the second tier Sym.simp already has for eq_def unfolding.* #15378\nfixes the pushNot simproc of the grind normalizer so that ¬(p → q) becomes p ∧ ¬q and ¬∀ x, p x becomes ∃ x, ¬p x. These rewrites have been dead code since #9271: the simproc matched the forallE case against the whole ¬p instead of p, and checked the domain with the syntactic Expr.isProp, which is false for a proposition variable. The same isProp check is fixed in the Sym.simp-based normalizer.* #15377\nremoves the grind = attribute from the lemmas that are already [grind hom] rules, so that grind derives the toNat, toInt and val image of a term once, through the homomorphism rewriter, instead of twice.* #15370\nmakes grind prove goals that mention the Bool-valued signed comparisons BitVec.slt and BitVec.sle, such as (x < y) ↔ (x.toBitVec.slt y.toBitVec = true) for Int64, x.slt y = true ↔ x.toInt < y.toInt, x.slt y → y.sle z → x.slt z and x.slt y = !(y.sle x).* #15369\nmakes grind prove goals about the signed value of a bitwise complement, such as (~~~x).toInt = (~~~x.toInt).bmod (2 ^ 16) for BitVec 16 and the corresponding goals for the fixed-width signed integer types, as well as plain Int facts such as ~~~a = -a - 1.* #15367\nmakes grind prove goals about the signed value of a bit vector shifted left by a variable amount, such as (x <<< n).toInt = (x.toInt <<< n).bmod (2 ^ 16), and lets nested signed results compose, such as ((x <<< m) <<< n).toInt = (x.toInt * 2 ^ m * 2 ^ n).bmod (2 ^ 16), ((x + y) <<< n).toInt = ((x.toInt + y.toInt) * 2 ^ n).bmod (2 ^ 16) and ((x * y) * z).toInt = (x.toInt * y.toInt * z.toInt).bmod (2 ^ 16).* #15366\nmakes simp [f] and dsimp [f] in sym => mode behave like the default simplifier when f is a definition or a conditional theorem. Side conditions of conditional theorems, such as the equational theorems of definitions with overlapping patterns, are now discharged using grind. Non-recursive definitions are unfolded when no equational theorem applies. dsimp accepts definitions and rfl-theorems. Reducible definitions are ignored with a warning instead of failing.* #15363\nfixes a grind failure on goals containing -0 : Int, such as f (-((0 : Nat) : Int)) = f 0, where grind produced a proof rejected by the kernel. -0 is now normalized to 0.* #15365\nmakes grind relate the unsigned value of an arithmetic shift to the signed value of its operand: goals such as ((x.sshiftRight n).toNat : Int) = (x.toInt / 2 ^ n) % 2 ^ 16 over BitVec 16 are now proved.* #15364\nmakes grind reason about bit-vector rotations through its [grind hom] injection into Nat: goals such as (x.rotateLeft n).toNat = (x.toNat * 2 ^ (n % 16) + x.toNat / 2 ^ (16 - n % 16)) % 2 ^ 16 over BitVec 16, and the rotateRight counterpart, are now proved.* #15362\nfixes Sym.DSimp.zetaDelta and Sym.DSimp.zetaDeltaAll so that they unfold a let-bound variable that appears as the head of an application. Previously, dsimp [foo] and dsimp [*] in grind/sym, and the bv_decide normalization passes, left terms such as foo a b untouched when foo := fun x y => x + y was a local definition, while Meta.DSimp reduces them to a + b.* #15350\nmakes grind reason about bit-vector exponentiation through its [grind hom] injections: goals such as (x.pow z).toNat = x.toNat ^ z % 2 ^ 16 and (x.pow z).toInt = (x.toInt ^ z).bmod (2 ^ 16) over BitVec 16 are now proved.* #15348\nmakes grind reason about bit-vector concatenation through its [grind hom] injection into Nat: goals such as (x ++ y).toNat = x.toNat * 2 ^ 8 + y.toNat over BitVec 8 are now proved. It also fixes Sym.simp rules whose left-hand side has Nat arithmetic in a type index, such as the width m + n of (x ++ y).toNat, which never fired on terms whose width had been normalized to a numeral.* #15347\nlets the cutsat solver in grind reorder its variables more than once during a proof search. Variables created after the first model search, e.g. by later E-matching rounds, were appended to the variable order; when such a variable has a large coefficient in its bound constraints, the Cooper case split that eliminates it enumerated that coefficient and exhausted liaSteps. For example, (x <<< 1).toInt = (x.toInt <<< 1).bmod (2 ^ 16) over BitVec 16 failed because the quotient x.toNat / 2 ^ 16, introduced by the instance of BitVec.toInt_eq_toNat_bmod, ended up last in the order with a coefficient of 2 ^ 16. Such goals are now proved.* #15345\nadds support for &&& with a constant mask to grind, for masks of the form 1…10…0 (a run of ones followed by a run of zeros), over Nat and, through the [grind hom] injections, BitVec and the fixed-width integer types. The mask is rewritten into %, /, and * by literals, which cutsat supports: x &&& (2^n - 1) becomes x % 2^n, and x &&& ((2^n - 1) * 2^k) becomes x / 2^k % 2^n * 2^k. For example, (x &&& 0xe0).toNat = x.toNat / 32 % 8 * 32 over BitVec 8 and x &&& 0xC0 = 0 → x &&& 0x40 = 0 over UInt8 are now proved by grind. Masks with holes (e.g. 0b101) and Int are not handled yet.* #15344\nmakes Sym.Arith.normalize? (and hence the arith simproc of Sym.simp) cancel x * x⁻¹ for an atom x when a discharger proves x ≠ 0, in any field. a * b / a becomes b, a ^ 3 / a becomes a ^ 2, and a / (2 * a) becomes 2⁻¹. The simproc DSL accepts arith with d, so arith with grind uses the goal's hypotheses. A result that asked for a side condition is context-dependent.* #15343\nmakes numeral inverses rational coefficients in Sym.Arith.normalize? (and hence in the arith simproc of Sym.simp) for fields of characteristic zero. a / 2 + a / 2 now normalizes to a, a / 2 + b / 3 to (3 * a + 2 * b) * 6⁻¹, and relations become denominator-free: a / 2 + b / 3 = 1 becomes 3 * a + 2 * b = 6. Without an IsCharP _ 0 instance, numeral inverses remain atoms.* #15259\nregisters BitVec.toNat_ofInt as a [grind hom] rule, so grind can reason about the unsigned value of BitVec.ofInt. In particular, cutsat can now follow carries computed through BitVec.ofInt.* #15262\nregisters BitVec.toInt_signExtend as a [grind hom] rule, so grind can reason about the signed value of BitVec.signExtend: narrowing reduces toInt modulo 2 ^ v, and widening leaves it unchanged.* #15311\nfixes grind never activating E-matching theorems whose ground pattern contains a lambda, a dependent ∀, or a nonparametric literal such as a Char or a negative Int. For example, @[grind .] theorem myPred_of_true : myPred (fun _ => True) was never used even when the goal contained myPred mySet and mySet = fun _ => True, and @[grind =] theorem fc_a : fc 'a' = 1 could not prove fc 'a' = 1. Passing such theorems explicitly with grind [myPred_of_true] worked, since explicit parameters bypass symbol indexing.* #15310\nfixes Sym.dsimp producing terms with dangling free variables when a binder type depends on an earlier binder, e.g. ∀ k : Nat, k < 32 → .... This caused bv_decide to fail with unknown free variable. Closes #15195.* #15046\nfixes Lean.Meta.Sym.Simp.toHave, which passed the dependencies of a have to Expr.betaRev in forward order even though betaRev expects them reversed. Any have whose value depended on two or more earlier haves of the same telescope was rebuilt with its dependencies swapped, producing an ill-typed proof term that the kernel rejects with an application type mismatch.* #14827\nmakes vcgen state exception postcondition verification conditions pointwise for more exception postcondition types, and makes throws clauses work for custom types. For Except ε, a spec with the exception postcondition fun e => e = \"x\" now yields ∀ e, e = \"x\" → … in place of an entailment between two functions. A custom type such as structure Thrown where onThrow : String → Prop gets the same pointwise verification conditions from a ToEStack instance, which converts it to an exception postcondition stack. With an EPostSlot instance, the type also supports throws clauses of def contracts.* #15290\nmoves the syntax of the vcgen tactic to the new module Std.WP.Tactic and deprecates the tactics of the Std.Do proof mode. import Std.WP now provides vcgen. Each call of a proof mode tactic, such as mintro or mspec, reports one deprecation warning that points to vcgen. The option mvcgen.warning is deprecated because it has no effect.* #15298\nteaches Sym.Arith.normalize? (and hence the arith simproc of Sym.simp) the field rewrites that need no side conditions. In a Field, a / b becomes a * b⁻¹, and inverses are pushed down to the atoms: (a * b)⁻¹ ↦ a⁻¹ * b⁻¹, (-a)⁻¹ ↦ -a⁻¹, a⁻¹⁻¹ ↦ a, 0⁻¹ ↦ 0, 1⁻¹ ↦ 1, after normalizing the term under the inverse. x⁻¹ for an atom or numeral x is then an atom of the polynomial, so a / b + a / b normalizes to 2 * (a * b⁻¹).* #15297\nextends Sym.Arith.normalize? (and hence the arith simproc of Sym.simp) to non-commutative Ring and Semiring structures. Monomials keep the order of their factors, so a * b and b * a stay distinct and (a + b)^2 normalizes to a^2 + a*b + b*a + b^2. Terms, relations, and the characteristic are supported as for the commutative case.* #15296\nfixes the arith simproc of Sym.simp so that reflexive relations between atoms are closed: x ≤ x and x = x now normalize to 0 ≤ 0 and 0 = 0 and are closed by post, like x + 0 ≤ x already was.* #15295\nmakes the relation normalizer of Sym.Arith (and hence the arith simproc of Sym.simp) use the characteristic of the ring, so that 256 * u + v = v on UInt8 normalizes to 0 = 0 and u + 2 = v is recognized as already normal. Previously only terms were reduced modulo the characteristic.* #15293\nextends Sym.Arith.normalize? (and hence the arith simproc of Sym.simp) to relations lhs = rhs, lhs ≤ rhs, and lhs < rhs over a CommRing or CommSemiring. Everything is moved to one side and split by sign, so x + y = z + 2 * x becomes y = x + z, and a ≤ b + a becomes 0 ≤ b; when both sides normalize to the same polynomial, the goal is closed.* #15292\nadds the arith simproc to Sym.simp, which normalizes ring and semiring terms into polynomial normal form using Sym.Arith.normalize?. It is opt-in through register_sym_simp and intended as a pre simproc: it sees the maximal arithmetic subtree first, simplifies its atoms with the full simplifier, and reifies it once. Normal forms are final (done := true) and cached.* #15280\nmakes Expr.toPolyS and Expr.toPolyS_nc handle a zero exponent like Expr.toPoly does, so x ^ 0 in a semiring normalizes to 1 instead of a monomial with exponent 0. The Sym.Arith normalizer now rewrites x ^ 0 : Nat to 1.* #15279\nadds Sym.Arith.normalize?, which rewrites a term of a CommRing or CommSemiring into polynomial normal form with a proof by reflection. It is the normalizer that Sym.simp will use for arithmetic; this PR provides the SymM-level API and its tests, not the simplifier integration.* #15267\nfixes regressions in bv_decide around projection and match reduction.* #15263\nmoves the classification of order structures (LE, IsPreorder, LT, LawfulOrderLT, IsPartialOrder, IsLinearPreorder, OrderedRing) from grind's order module into Sym.Arith, so other Sym components can reuse it. No user-visible change.* #15190\nmakes the grind ring solver use the ring classification, operator caches, reification, and denotation from Sym.Arith, and deletes the copies it kept under Grind/Arith/CommRing. A type is now classified once per SymM run instead of once per goal, so every goal of a grind run and every tactic of a sym block share the result. Code that drives grind programmatically sees the new layout: the per-goal ring state holds solver data only, Result carries the Sym.Arith ring records, and goalToMessageData and evalCheck take them.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Release Notes\u0009Lean 4.36.0 (2026-11-16)","header":"Tactics","id":"/releases/v4.36.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___36___0-_LPAR_2026-11-16_RPAR_--Tactics"}});